a popular stretch of beaches in  Massachusetts will remain close to  swimming today because of a deadly  weekend shark attack 26 year old arthur  medici died saturday after being bitten  by a shark it was the first deadly shark  attack in Massachusetts in more than 80  years the attack happened on a stretch  of the Cape Cod National Seashore  visitors are being told to stay out of  the water  Nicky batiste is in Wellfleet  Massachusetts near the scene of the  attack Nicky good morning good morning  on Saturday around noon arthur medici  and his friend were riding waves on  their boogie boards about 30 yards out  into this water right off this Cape Cod  Shore that's when one witness says he  saw what looked like a 10 to 12-foot  shark attack Medici Waldman was just  going like this waving to us and I ran  up more than a dozen beachgoers on Cape  Cod Saturday scrambled to help carry  arthur medici to safety after he was  bitten on both legs by a shark let's  bring it back up the beach screaming  like a lunatic call 9-1-1 has been a  shark attack a report of an unknown  shark bite medici was rushed to a nearby  hospital where he was later pronounced  dead isaac rota was in the water near  medici when the attack happened he says  he and the victim were like brothers  he was screaming and then uh I saw like  a shark trailer and I swam him fastest I  couldn't have moment I dragged him back  to shore and I got a boogie board  strapped and I turn around to the side  to try to stop the bleeding saturday's  shark attack is at least the second on  Cape Cod this summer in August sixty  one-year-old bill Linton survived after  being bitten by a shark after two  attacks I think we can be concerned that  it's a dangerous situation we see these  sharks every day along this coastline  they are there in big numbers and and  people have to be vigilant and be aware  of it Greg skomal is a marine biologist  for the state of Massachusetts he  believes the shark likely mistook Medici  for a seal which are often close to  shore along Cape Cod's beaches and as a  result these sharks are coming as close  as they possibly can without risking  their own lives to try to kill and eat  the  seals and and that creates a bit of a  problem because that's exactly where  people are utilizing the shoreline and  so you know this is something we're  looking at it's something we're trying  to advise swimmers about so that this  does not happen again the National Park  Service is warning people on Cape Cod to  not swim with the seals and we've seen a  few seals just a few yards offshore here  this morning while shark attacks are  rare last year one study says there were  53 unprovoked shark attacks in the u.s.  that's more than any other country in  the world  John Nikki thanks
 

